# Sequelize Query Conventions

Build every query with Sequelize ORM features (`include`, association joins, `fn`, `col`, `where`, `Op`, `subQuery`). Never write raw SQL or `Sequelize.literal()` unless there is no ORM alternative and it is explicitly approved.

## Tenant scoping
- Read/write tenant-owned models through `scoped(model.x)` (from `utility/scoped.js`), not `model.x` directly.
- When scoping a joined association, add `where: buildScope(model.x)` on that `include`.

```js
// GOOD
return scoped(model.timeTableRoutineModel).findOne({ where: { timeTableRoutineId }, attributes });

// GOOD — scope on a join
include: [{ model: model.timeTableRoutineModel, as: 'timeTablecreate', required: true, where: buildScope(model.timeTableRoutineModel) }]
```

## Attributes & includes
- Always pass explicit `attributes` (list or `{ exclude: [...] }`); never select all columns implicitly.
- Reuse shared include builders (e.g. `routineStructureInclude`, `timeTableRoutineClassSectionInclude`) instead of re-declaring nested joins inline.
- Set `required: true/false` intentionally to control INNER vs LEFT joins.

## Filtering & dates
- Use `Op` for all operators; build conditional `where` with plain objects and `if` guards.
- Compare DATE-only columns with `Sequelize.where(Sequelize.fn('DATE', Sequelize.col(...)), { [Op.lte]: date })` to avoid UTC shifts.

## Writes
- Thread `transaction` through every create/update/delete that participates in a multi-step operation.
- Coerce external ids with `Number(id)` before querying.
